\begin_layout Section
Accounting for Multiple Inference
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Accounting-for-Multiple"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As cash transfers are likely to impact a large number of economic behaviors
 and dimensions of welfare, and given that our survey instrument often included
 several questions related to a single behavior or dimension, we account
 for multiple hypotheses by using outcome variable indices and family-wise
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value adjustment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have catalogued in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Components-of-indices"

\end_inset

 the primary indices that we consider in the analysis.
 For each of these indices, we will report both unadjusted 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values as well as 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values corrected for multiple comparisons using the Family-Wise Error Rate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Construction of indices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To keep the number of outcome variables low and thus allow for greater statistic
al power even after adjusting 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values for multiple inference, we construct indices for several of our
 groups of outcome variables.
 To this end, we follow the procedure proposed by Anderson 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{anderson_multiple_2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

, which is reproduced below: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, for each outcome variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{jk}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 indexes the outcome group and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 indexes variables within outcome groups, we re-code the variable such that
 high values correspond to positive outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then compute the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}_{j}}$
\end_inset

 for outcomes in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, which consists of elements:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Sigma}_{jmn}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{jmn}}\frac{y_{ijm}-\bar{y}_{jm}}{\sigma_{jm}^{y}}\frac{y_{ijn}-\bar{y}_{jn}}{\sigma_{jn}^{y}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{jmn}$
\end_inset

 is the number of non-missing observations for outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{jm}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{jn}$
\end_inset

 are the means for outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, respectively, in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{jm}^{y}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{jn}^{y}$
\end_inset

 are the standard deviations in the pure control group for the same outcomes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next, we invert the covariance matrix, and define weight 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{jk}$
\end_inset

 for each outcome 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 by summing the entries in the row of the inverted covariance matrix correspondi
ng to that outcome: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}_{j}^{-1}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
c_{j11} & c_{j12} & \cdots & c_{j1K}\\
c_{j21} & c_{j22} & \cdots & \cdots\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \ddots\\
c_{jK1} & \vdots & \ddots & c_{jKK}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{jk}=\sum_{l=1}^{K_{j}}c_{jkl}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of outcome variables in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, we transform each outcome variable by subtracting its mean and
 dividing by the control group standard deviation, and then weighting it
 with the weights obtained as described above.
 We denote the result 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{ij}$
\end_inset

 because this transformation yields a generalized least squares estimator
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortcite{anderson_multiple_2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_{ij}=\left(\sum_{k\in\mathbbm{K}_{ij}}w_{jk}\right)^{-1}\sum_{k\in\mathbbm{K}_{ij}}w_{jk}\frac{y_{ijk}-\bar{y}_{jk}}{\sigma_{jk}^{y}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbbm{K}_{ij}$
\end_inset

 denotes the set of non-missing outcomes for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in outcome group 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Family-wise Error Rate 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Family-wise-Error-Rate"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because combining individual outcome variables in indices as described above
 still leaves us with multiple outcome variables (viz.
 separate index variables for health, education, etc.), we additionally adjust
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values of our coefficients of interest for multiple statistical inference.
 These coefficients are those on the treatment dummies in the basic specificatio
ns, or those on the dummies for individual treatment arms.
 To this end, we proceed as follows, reproduced again from Anderson 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{anderson_multiple_2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 A similar procedure is described in Lee & Shaikh 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{lee_multiple_2013}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and Romano & Wolf 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{romano_exact_2005}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, we compute naïve 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values for all index variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{j}$
\end_inset

 of our 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 main outcome groups, and sort these 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values in ascending order, i.e.
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}<p_{2}<\cdots<p_{J}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, we follow Anderson's 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{anderson_multiple_2008}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 variant of Efron & Tibshirani's 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citeyear{efron_introduction_1993}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 non-parametric permutation test: for each index variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{j}$
\end_inset

 of our 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 main outcome groups, we randomly permute the treatment assignments across
 the entire sample, and estimate the model of interest to obtain the 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value for the coefficient of interest.
 We enforce monotonicity in the resulting vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values 
\begin_inset Formula $[p_{1}^{*},\,p_{2}^{*},\,\cdots\,p_{J}^{*}]'$
\end_inset

 by computing 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{r}^{**}=\min\{p_{r}^{*},\,p_{r+1}^{*},\,\cdots\,p_{J}^{*}\}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 is the position of the outcome in the vector of naïve 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then repeat this procedure 1,000 times.
 The non-parametric 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{r}^{fwer*}$
\end_inset

, for each outcome is the fraction of iterations on which the simulated
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value is smaller than the observed 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-value.
 Finally we enforce monotonicity again: 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{r}^{fwer}=\min\{p_{r}^{fwer*},\,p_{r+1}^{fwer*},\,\cdots\,p_{J}^{fwer*}\}$
\end_inset

.
 This yields the final vector of family-wise error-rate corrected 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values.
 We will report both these 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values and the naïve 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values.
 Within outcome groups, we report naïve 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

-values for individual outcome variables other than the indices.
\end_layout

