\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:selection_criteria_analysis-1"

\end_inset

Adjusting for thatched roof selection criterion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A potential weakness in conducting a spillover analysis in the present study
 is that the thatched-roof selection criterion for participation was applied
 to households in control villages one year after it was applied to households
 in treatment villages.
 As a result, there is endogenous selection into the pure control condition,
 as some proportion of households in pure control villages are likely to
 have upgraded to a metal roof over this time period, and these households
 are excluded from endline in the pure control villages.
 These households are potentially different both from households that did
 not upgrade, and different from households in treatment villages that only
 upgraded in response to their neighbors receiving transfers.
 Thus, the fact that no metal roof households are included in the endline
 survey of control villages potentially biases the spillover analysis.
 In the following, we describe the selection problem formally, and outline
 the analyses we perform to bound any resulting bias.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Basic Selection Problem 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We treat the bias introduced by the time lag in the application of the thatched
 roof criterion as selection bias.
 Consider the following sample selection model (cf.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteNP{angrist2006}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteNP{lee2009}
\end_layout

\end_inset

):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
D & =\mbox{Assignment to treatment village}\\
S & =\mbox{Household takes the endline survey}\\
S_{1} & =\mbox{Household would take the endline survey if assigned to spillover status}\\
S_{0} & =\mbox{Household would take the endline survey if assigned to pure control status}\\
Y & =\mbox{Outcome of interest}\\
Y_{1} & =\mbox{Outcome of interest if assigned to spillover status }\\
Y_{0} & =\mbox{Outcome of interest if assigmed to pure control status}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the sample is restricted to
\emph on
 control
\emph default
 households (in both treatment and pure control villages), and the treatment
 dummy 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 identifies 
\emph on
spillover
\emph default
 households, i.e.
 control households in treatment villages.
 For now we abstract away from selection bias through attrition and consider
 only bias from differential application of the thatched roof eligibility
 criterion.
 For each individual, we only observe one of the sample selection indicators
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Similarly, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{1}$
\end_inset

 are latent potential outcomes that we only observe if an individual takes
 the endline survey.
 Thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
S & =S_{1}D+S_{0}(1-D)\\
Y & =S\left[Y_{1}D+Y_{0}(1-D)\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Randomization gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{0},Y_{1},S_{0},S_{1}\perp D$
\end_inset

.
 Calculating the spillover effect from the observed sample gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=1\right]-E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=0\right]$
\end_inset

.
 However, this is a biased measure of the average effect of living in a
 treatment village for individuals who were observed:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
 & E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=1\right]-E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=0\right]\\
 & =E\left[Y_{1}\mid S_{1}=1,\,D=1\right]-E\left[Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1,\,D=0\right]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}\mid S_{1}=1]-E[Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}\mid S_{1}=1]+E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1]-E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1]-E[Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]+E[Y_{1}\mid S_{1}=1]-E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]+E[Y_{1}]-E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The term 
\begin_inset Formula $E[Y_{1}]-E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1]$
\end_inset

 identifies any bias arising from the fact that individuals who did not
 upgrade their roofs (and thus were eligible to be surveyed) in pure control
 villages may have different outcomes from those who do.
 Also note that we use 
\begin_inset Formula $E[Y_{1}\mid S_{1}=1]=E[Y_{1}]$
\end_inset

, since all households in treatment villages are observed.
 We perform the following analyses to bound this selection effect.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Spillover effect including metal roof households 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 1: Random selection into roof upgrade
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin by assuming that selection into roof upgrade (and hence out of
 the endline survey in the pure control villages) is random, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\perp S_{0}$
\end_inset

, and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[Y_{1}]=E[Y_{1}|S_{1}=1]=E[Y_{1}|S_{0}=1].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This assumption allows us to identify the treatment effect through the simple
 comparison of all households that took the endline survey, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]=E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=1\right]-E\left[Y\mid S=1,\,D=0\right]$
\end_inset

.
 We refer to this as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

naïve
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 analysis.
 Results are given Table III and Section 4.2 of the paper.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One source of evidence for the comparability of the spillover and pure control
 samples is to compare them on baseline characteristics.
 However, no baseline survey was administered to pure control households.
 Nevertheless, there some individual and household characteristics are either
 immutable or calculable from endline values.
 We compare the two types of households on these characteristics in Section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Baseline-Balance-on-immutables"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a further source of evidence in support of this assumption, we would
 ideally ask whether selection into upgrade can be predicted from baseline
 observables in the pure control group; however, we do not have data on
 the metal roof households.
 A second-best option is to ask whether selection into upgrade can be predicted
 from baseline observables in the spillover group.
 We present this analysis and its results in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Determinants-of-Metal"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Controlling for baseline characteristics 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 2: Random selection into roof upgrade conditional
 on observables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We next assume that selection of spillover and pure control households into
 upgrade (and hence the endline survey) is random conditional on a set of
 observable household characteristics 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 In this case, the conditional independence assumption holds, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\perp S_{0}\mid X$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, if we control for these covariates in our specification, we can identify
 the treatment effect: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
 & E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1,X]+E[Y_{1}|S_{1}=1,X]-E[Y_{1}\mid S_{0}=1,X]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1,X]+E[Y_{1}\mid X]-E[Y_{1}\mid X]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1,X]\\
 & =E[Y_{1}-Y_{0}\mid S_{0}=1]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last equality is true because 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\perp S_{0}\mid X$
\end_inset

 by assumption.
 Thus, we can recalculate the spillover effect using baseline characteristics
 as control variables.
 The specification and results are given in Section 4.2 of the paper.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Restricting the sample to households with thatched roofs at endline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now consider improvements in identification resulting from restricting
 the sample to households which still have thatched roofs at endline.
 To begin, we define notation for never-takers, always-takers, compliers,
 and defiers of metal roof upgrade between baseline and endline.
 Note that again the sample is restricted to households in the spillover
 and pure control groups, and therefore a complier household is one that
 upgrades to a metal roof as the result of a spillover effect from neighboring
 households receiving a transfer; a defier household is one that does not
 upgrade for this reason.
 We denote actual and potential roof status at endline as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
R & =\mbox{Household upgrades to metal roof between baseline and endline}\\
R_{1} & =\mbox{Household would upgrade to metal roof if assigned to spillover status}\\
R_{0} & =\mbox{Household would upgrade to metal roof if assigned to pure control status}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We further denote the proportions of always-takers, compliers, defiers,
 and never-takers as follows: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned}\text{Always-takers: } & Pr(R_{0}=1,R_{1}=1)=\alpha\\
\text{Compliers: } & Pr(R_{0}=0,R_{1}=1)=\gamma\\
\text{Defiers: } & Pr(R_{0}=1,R_{1}=0)=\phi\\
\text{Never-takers: } & Pr(R_{0}=0,R_{1}=0)=\nu
\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now consider the comparison of spillover and pure control households which
 still have thatched roofs at endline.
 In treatment villages, the households with thatched roofs at endline are
 either defiers or never-takers.
 In pure control villages, they are either compliers or never-takers.
 The difference between the observed and potential outcomes can therefore
 be written and rearranged as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{aligned} & \nu E[Y_{N}|S=1,D=1]+\phi E[Y_{F}|S=1,D=1]-\nu E[Y_{N}|S=1,D=0]-\gamma E[Y_{C}|S=1,D=0]\\
 & =\nu E[Y_{N,1}|S_{1}=1,D=1]+\phi E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1,D=1]-\nu E[Y_{N,0}|S_{0}=1,D=0]-\gamma E[Y_{C,0}|S_{0}=1,D=0]\\
 & =\nu E[Y_{N,1}|S_{1}=1]+\phi E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1]-\nu E[Y_{N,0}|S_{0}=1]-\gamma E[Y_{C,0}|S_{0}=1]\\
 & =\nu E[Y_{N,1}|S_{1}=1]+\phi E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1]-\nu E[Y_{N,0}|S_{0}=1]-\gamma E[Y_{C,0}|S_{0}=1]\\
 & \,\,\,+\nu E[Y_{N,1}|S_{0}=1]-\nu E[Y_{N,1}|S_{0}=1]\\
 & =\nu E[Y_{N,1}-Y_{N,0}|S_{0}=1]+\phi E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1]-\gamma E[Y_{C,1}|S_{0}=1]
\end{aligned}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the difference between households with thatched roofs at endline is
 identified for never-takers, except for the difference between the proportion
 and potential outcomes of households that are compliers or defiers in terms
 of upgrading to metal roofs.
 We next outline under which assumptions this bias is zero or can be bounded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 3: Monotonicity (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no defiers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We first make the classic monotonicity or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no defiers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 assumption that is at the foundation of many randomized field experiments
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortcite{angrist1996}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 In our framework, the assumption states that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi=0$
\end_inset

.
 Could there be defiers in our sample? In our view, the only plausible reason
 for control households to refrain from upgrading their thatched roofs to
 metal is to remain eligible for possible future transfers from 
\emph on
GiveDirectly
\emph default
.
 However, control households in treatment villages were credibly told by
 
\emph on
GiveDirectly
\emph default
 that they would not receive cash transfers.
 The no-defier assumption is therefore reasonable in our setting.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this assumption, the only bias arises from compliers, which are included
 in the pure control thatched-roof sample but not in the spillover thatched-roof
 sample because they upgraded to metal roofs.
 Importantly, we can find out how many such households there are by obtaining
 a precise estimate of the magnitude of the spillover effect of the cash
 transfers on metal roof ownership.
 In September 2015, we returned to households with metal roofs in pure control
 villages to ascertain when they upgraded to a metal roof.
 Households that upgraded between April 2011 and June 2012 should originally
 have been eligible for participation in the study, but were excluded because
 of the late application of the thatched roof criterion.
 We identified 170 such households.
 We then used the same algorithm originally used to select pure control
 households to calculate the probability that each of these households would
 have been included in the study had they been identified as eligible at
 the time.
 The original sampling method required us to select 8 households from the
 pool of eligible households in each village (those with thatched roofs).
 When there were 8 or fewer eligible households in a given village, we selected
 all households.
 When there more than 8 eligible households, we selected 8 with equal probabilit
y for each.
 We were thus able to calculate the exact probability that a given household
 would be selected in each village.
 In villages with 8 or fewer eligible households, the probability of selection
 was 1.
 In villages with more than 8 eligible households, the probability was 8
 divided by the total number of eligible households.
 To determine how many of the 170 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

recall
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 households should have been selected for the survey, we multiply this probabili
ty by the number of recall households in each village, resulting in a total
 of 78 households.
 Since there were 432 pure control households in the original study, this
 gives us an upgrade rate from baseline to endline of 78/(432 + 78) = 0.153
 for pure control villages.
 Similarly, since there were a total of 469 spillover households at endline,
 of which 77 had metal roofs, the upgrade rate among spillover households
 was 77/469 = 0.164.
 Applying the upgrade rate of 0.153 in pure control village to these spillover
 households, we would predict 
\begin_inset Formula $0.153\cdot469=72$
\end_inset

 metal roofs in the spillover group at endline.
 In actuality we observe 77 metal roof households.
 The treatment therefore had a spillover effect on metal roof ownership
 of  of 77 – 72 = 5 households.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We take two approaches to the bias arising from these five households.
 The first is to ignore it: with 5 households our of 469, i.e.
 1.1 percent, the spillover effect of transfers on metal roof ownership is
 negligible.
 We can therefore consider the spillover analysis that restricts the sample
 to households that still have thatched roof at endline as nearly uncontaminated
 by spillover effects on metal roof ownership.
 In this case, restricting the sample to households that still have thatch
 roofs at endline identifies the spillover effect.
 The second approach is to bound the spillover effect using worst-case assumptio
ns.
 We therefore report Lee and Manski bounds in Table III of the paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 4A: Same proportion and potential outcomes for compliers
 and defiers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now relax the monotonicity assumption and ask under which alternative
 assumptions the comparison of thatch-at-endline households in treatment
 and control villages identifies the spillover effect.
 One such assumption is that the proportion and potential outcomes of compliers
 and defiers are the same, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1]=\gamma E[Y_{C,1}|S_{0}=1].
\]

\end_inset

This assumption says that the proportion and outcome distribution of households
 which are induced to upgrade to metal roofs when their neighbors receive
 transfers are identical to those of households which are induced to keep
 their thatched roofs by treatment.
 That the outcome distribution of these two types is similar is plausible
 because both types are marginal, i.e.
 they are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

ready to upgrade
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 before transfers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 4B: Same potential outcomes for compliers and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\gamma}{\phi}$
\end_inset

 of the defiers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A weaker assumption is that only 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\gamma}{\phi}$
\end_inset

 of the defiers have the same potential outcomes as the compliers.
 This leaves a proportion of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi-\gamma$
\end_inset

 of the sample whose outcome distribution we don't know and who therefore
 contaminate the spillover effect estimate.
 However, from the exercise described above, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi-\gamma=0.011$
\end_inset

.
 Again, this is negligible and can either be ignored, our bounded as described
 above.
 The details of this approach have been described by 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteN{angrist1996}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
shortciteN{dechaisemartin2013}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Identifying assumption 4C: Same potential outcomes for compliers, never-takers,
 and defiers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, we can relax the assumption that the proportion of compliers and
 defiers are the same if we instead assume that their distribution of potential
 outcomes is the same as that of the never-takers, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $E[Y_{F,1}|S_{1}=1]=E[Y_{C,1}|S_{0}=1]=E[Y_{N,1}|S_{0}=1]$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption says that the spillover group, which consists of never-takers
 and defiers, has the same outcome distribution as the pure control group,
 which consists of never-takers and defiers.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Testing whether inclusion vs.
 exclusion of metal roof households affects results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We next ask whether including vs.
 excluding households with metal roofs at endline from the spillover analysis
 affects results.
 To this end, we analyze the difference in spillover effects when calculated
 across all spillover households and when excluding spillover households
 that upgraded.
 If we find that the results are similar whether or not we exclude metal
 roof households, this suggests that the differential application of the
 thatched roof criterion introduced only minimal bias.
 We estimate a series of models of the form: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{vhiE,m}=\beta_{m}Sp_{vh}+\varepsilon_{vhiE,m}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 denotes the model number and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{vhiE,m}$
\end_inset

 is an idiosyncratic error term.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 (or 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for individual measures) indexes either the total number of spillover household
s or the number of spillover households that did not upgrade:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $m=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $h=1...H_{1}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of spillover households and pure control households.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $m=2$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $h=1...H_{2}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the total number of spillover households that did not upgrade and pure
 control households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Writing each specification in vector form and stacking, we get the seemingly
 unrelated regression model: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\begin{array}{c}
Y_{1}\\
Y_{2}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{cc}
Sp_{1} & 0\\
0 & Sp_{2}
\end{array}\right)\left(\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{1}\\
\beta_{2}
\end{array}\right)+\left(\begin{array}{c}
\varepsilon_{1}\\
\varepsilon_{2}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Standard errors are clustered at the village level.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{m}$
\end_inset

 identifies the spillover effect in model 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 After estimating this specification, we test for the equality of each 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 If we cannot reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\beta_{1}=\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

, this again suggests that spillover households that upgraded are not significan
tly different in terms of the outcome variable to those that did not upgrade,
 and that the late application of the exclusion restriction introduced minimal
 bias into our calculation of the spillover effect.
 The results are presented in Table III and Section 4.2 of the paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
