\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Distributional-effects"

\end_inset

Distributional effects 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, we are concerned with the distributional impact of the
 transfers.
 In particular, we consider whether the average impacts described in the
 main paper are the result of shifting particular portions of the distribution
 of that outcome, and, where no average impact is observed, whether the
 lack of an average impact may mask shifts in specific portions of the distribut
ion for outcomes.
 To this end, we run quantile regressions for the outcomes of interest.
 In particular, we estimate the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{q}$
\end_inset

 that minimizes the following expression:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i:y_{vhi}\geqq T_{vh}\beta_{q}}q|y_{vhi}-T_{vh}\beta_{q}|+\sum_{i:y_{vhi}\leqq T_{vh}\beta_{q}}(1-q)|y_{vhi}-T_{vh}\beta_{q}|\label{eq:2-2-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{q}$
\end_inset

 we again restrict the sample to treatment and control households within
 treatment villages.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{q}$
\end_inset

 thus estimates the within-village treatment effect on quantile 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 
\emph default
of the distribution.
 In the results below, we present results for each decile in the outcome
 distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These results are shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:QRegs_indices"

\end_inset

, where we plot the parameter estimates for all deciles and their 95 percent
 confidence intervals.
 We note three patterns.
 First, the plots for assets, consumption, and cash flows from self-employment
 are strongly upward-sloping, suggesting that the treatment effects on these
 outcomes are strongest for wealthier households.
 Second, the plots for food security and psychological wellbeing show a
 treatment effect throughout the distribution, suggesting that cash transfers
 impact households at all levels of those particular measures of welfare.
 Finally, the plots for health, education, and female empowerment show no
 treatment effects anywhere in the distribution.
 
\end_layout

