\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:List-Randomization"

\end_inset

List Randomization for Alcohol and Tobacco Consumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, we report an analysis designed to account for any reporting
 bias in rates of socially undesirable behaviors – specifically alcohol
 and tobacco consumption.
 Individuals were presented with a list of common activities and asked how
 many of these activities they had participated in during the preceding
 week.
 Individuals were randomly assigned to one of three conditions.
 In one condition, individuals were presented with 5 activities that would
 generally be considered neutral (e.g.
 used a phone, ate ugali).
 In a second condition, individuals were given a list including the same
 5 activities, but with the addition of a 6th: smoking at least one cigarette
 in the last week.
 Similarly, in the third condition, individuals were given a list with the
 basic 5 activities, plus drinking alcohol in the last week.
 Individuals were clearly instructed to provide only the total number of
 activities in which they had participated, not 
\emph on
which 
\emph default
of the activities, and were told that the enumerator was not supposed to
 know in
\emph on
 which
\emph default
 of the activities they had participated.
 Thus, this method should not be vulnerable to underreporting because of
 reputational concerns.
 Randomization of the lists was stratified such that 1/3 of treatment households
 and 1/3 of spillover households was presented with each type of list to
 allow us to identify the effect of treatment on smoking and alcohol consumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The treatment effect on tobacco and alcohol consumption can be found by
 comparing the mean number of activities in each of the three groups using
 the following specification.
 Note that we analyze the specification separately, by comparing the list
 including alcohol to the basic list, and by comparing the list including
 cigarettes to the basic list.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Activities_{vhiE}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}list_{vhi}+\beta_{2}list_{vhi}\times T_{vh}+\alpha_{v}+\varepsilon_{vhiE}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $Activities_{vhiE}$
\end_inset

 is the number of activities reported by individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 of village 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=\mbox{endline}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $list_{ihv}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if an individual was presented
 with the long list (either including alcohol or cigarettes depending on
 the analysis) and a 0 if the individual was presented with the basic list.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 was treated.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $list_{vhi}\times T_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 lives in a treatment household and was presented with the long list.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{v}$
\end_inset

 is a village-level fixed effect.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{vhiE}$
\end_inset

 is an idiosyncratic error term.
 Note that pure control households are excluded from this analysis.
 We calculate heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 represents the difference in means between the longer list and the basic
 lists, which can be interpreted as the overall prevalence of smoking tobacco
 or drinking alcohol in the sample population.
 Likewise, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 identifies any effect of treatment status on the likelihood of smoking
 or drinking.
 
\end_layout

