\begin_layout Section
Detailed Findings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Description of analyses and econometric specifications
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section we provide detailed results for the constituent elements
 of each of our primary outcome indices.
 Each of the following sections proceeds as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Primary treatment effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first table provides an overview of the primary treatment effects, as
 well as differential effects in each treatment arm.
 In each of these tables, column (1) reports the mean of a given outcome
 variable taken among control households in treatment villages.
 Column (2) reports the primary treatment within-village treatment effect,
 using the specification detailed in Equation 2 of the main paper.
 Column (3) reports the differential effect for households in which the
 primary female received the transfer compared to households in which the
 primary male received the transfer, evaluated using the specification in
 Equation 3 of the main paper.
 Column (4) reports the differential effect for households receiving a monthly
 transfers compared to households receiving a lump-sum payment, evaluated
 using the specification in Equation 4 of the main paper.
 Column (5) reports the differential effect for households receiving large
 transfers compared to households receiving small transfers.
 Column (6) reports the sample size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Primary treatment effects with baseline controls
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second table in each section adds additional controls for various baseline
 covariates: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+X\gamma'+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}t}\label{eq:primary_w_controls}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a vector of individual and household baseline characteristics.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use two sets of controls.
 First, we use demographic and baseline economic characteristics:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Demographic 
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Age of primary respondent at baseline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Marital status / household type (single vs.
 married) at baseline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Highest level of education attained by primary respondent at baseline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Number of children at baseline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Household size at baseline
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Economic
\begin_inset Separator latexpar
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Baseline consumption, asset levels, and land holdings at baseline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Ownership of non-agricultural enterprise at baseline 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Ownership of agricultural enterprise at baseline 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Participation in wage labor at baseline
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Across-village treatment effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this table, we detail treatment effects using an across-village comparison
 (rather than a within village comparison).
 Column (1) reports the mean of a given outcome variable taken among control
 households in treatment villages.
 Column (2) reports the within-village treatment effect using the specification
 reported in Equation 5 of the main paper: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}t}\label{eq:basic_within}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
restricting the analysis to treatment villages only.
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{vh\{i\}E}$
\end_inset

 is the outcome of interest for household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 in village 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, measured at endline (
\begin_inset Formula $t=E$
\end_inset

); index 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is included for outcomes measured at the level of the individual respondent,
 and omitted for outcomes measured at the household level.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is a treatment indicator that takes value 1 for households which received
 a cash transfer (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

treatment households
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) and 0 otherwise.
 We condition on the baseline level of the outcome variable when available,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{vh\{i\}B}$
\end_inset

, to improve statistical power.
 To include observations where the baseline outcome is missing, we code
 missing values as zero and include a dummy indicator that the variable
 is missing (
\begin_inset Formula $M_{vhiB}$
\end_inset

).
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{v}$
\end_inset

 is a village-level fixed effect.
 Standard errors are clustered at the household level.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 2) identifies the treatment effect from the within-village comparisons.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Column (3), we report the across-village treatment effect, and in column
 (4) we report the spillover effect, both measured using the following specifica
tion: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}+\beta_{2}S_{vh}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:basic_across}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $S_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is a dummy variable that takes value 1 for control households in treatment
 villages (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spillover households
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) and 0 otherwise.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}$
\end_inset

 is an idiosyncratic error term.
 The omitted category is control households in pure control villages (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

control households
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

).
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 identifies the treatment effect for treated households relative to control
 households in control villages (column 3), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 identifies within-village spillover effects by comparing control households
 in treatment villages to control households in pure control villages (column
 4).
 To account for possible correlation in outcomes within villages, the error
 term is clustered at the village level.
 Column (5) reports the sample size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Male vs.
 female recipients
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this table, we report various measures of the effects for treatment arms
 in which the primary male vs.
 the primary female received the transfer.
 In column (1), we report the mean of a given outcome variable among control
 households in treatment village.
 In Column (2) and (3) we report the effects for households in which the
 primary female and primary male received the transfer respectively.
 We exclude pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{F}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{M}}+\beta_{3}T_{vh}^{\text{W}}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:MF_within}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{x}}$
\end_inset

 are indicator functions that specify the branch of the different treatment
 arms.
 Specifically, they indicate whether the transfer recipient is female (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{F}}$
\end_inset

), male (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{M}}$
\end_inset

), or that the gender of the recipient could not be randomized because the
 household only had one head (most commonly in the case of widows/widowers)
 (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{W}}$
\end_inset

).
 Spillover households are the omitted category.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 2) identifies the effect of transfers to the primary female in
 comparison to spillover households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 3) identifies the effect of a transfer to the primary male in compariso
n to spillover households.
 In this specification, standard errors are clustered at the household level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Column (4), we report the comparison between treatment households in
 which the transfer was received by the primary female and households in
 which the treatment was received by the primary male.
 Again excluding pure control households, we evaluate 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{F}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{W}}+\beta_{3}S_{vh}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:MF_compare}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is a spillover household.
 Here the omitted category is treatment households in which the primary
 male received the transfers.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (reported in column 4) is the difference in the outcome for households
 in which the primary female received the transfer and households in which
 the primary female received the transfer.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Columns (5) and (6), we report the across-village treatment effect for
 households in which the transfer was received by the primary female and
 households in which the treatment was received by the primary male.
 In this analysis, we include pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{F}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{M}}+\beta_{3}T_{vh}^{\text{W}}+\beta_{4}S_{vh}+\beta_{5}PC_{vh}^{\text{SINGLE}}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:MF_across}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $PC_{vh}^{\text{SINGLE}}$
\end_inset

is an indicator for pure control households with a single head.
 Thus, the omitted category is cohabiting pure control households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 5) identifies the treatment effect when the primary female in the
 household receives the transfer.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 6) identifies the treatment effect when the primary male in the
 household receives the transfer.
 Note that standard errors are clustered at the village level in this analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Monthly vs.
 lump-sum recipients
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this table, we report various measures of the effects for monthly and
 lump-sum treatment arms.
 In column (1), we report the mean of a given outcome variable among control
 households in treatment village.
 In Column (2) and (3) we report the effect of receiving a monthly transfer
 and a lump-sum transfer respectively.
 We exclude pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{MTH}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{LS}}+\beta_{3}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:Monthly_within}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{x}}$
\end_inset

 are indicator functions that specify the branch of the different treatment
 arms.
 Specifically, they indicate whether the transfer recipient is monthly (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{MTH}}$
\end_inset

), lump-sum (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{LS}}$
\end_inset

), or that the household could not be categorized as either monthly or lump-sum
 because it received a large transfer (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}$
\end_inset

).
 Spillover households are the omitted category.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 2) identifies the effect of monthly transfers in comparison to
 spillover households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 3) identifies the effect of a lump-sum transfer tin comparison
 to spillover households.
 In this specification, standard errors are clustered at the household level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Column (4), we report the comparison between treatment households that
 received the transfer via a series of monthly payments versus those that
 received the transfer as a lump sum.
 Again excluding pure control households, we evaluate 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{MTH}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\beta_{3}S_{vh}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:Monthly_compare}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is a spillover household.
 Here the omitted category is households that received lump-sum transfers.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (reported in column 4) is the difference in the outcome for households
 that received monthly transfers versus those that received lump-sum transfers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Columns (5) and (6), we report the across-village treatment effect for
 monthly and lump-sum transfer.
 In this analysis, we include pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{MTH}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{LS}}+\beta_{3}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\beta_{4}S_{vh}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:Monthly_across}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the omitted category is pure control households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 5) identifies the effect of a monthly transfer using an across-village
 comparison.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 6) identifies the effect of a lump-sum transfer using an across-village
 comparison.
 Note that standard errors are clustered at the village level in this analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Large vs.
 small transfer recipients
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this table, we report the effects for treatment arms in which households
 received large versus small transfers.
 In column (1), we report the mean of a given outcome variable among control
 households in treatment village.
 In Column (2) and (3) we report the effect of receiving a large transfer
 and a small sum transfer respectively.
 We exclude pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{S}}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:LS_within}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{x}}$
\end_inset

 are indicator functions that specify the branch of the different treatment
 arms.
 Specifically, they indicate whether the transfer recipient is large (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{L}$
\end_inset

) or small (
\begin_inset Formula $T_{vh}^{\text{S}}$
\end_inset

) transfer.
 Note that small transfers encompasses both monthly and lump-sum conditions.
 Spillover households are the omitted category.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 2) identifies the effect of receiving a large transfers in comparison
 to spillover households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 3) identifies the effect of receiving a small transfer in comparison
 to spillover households.
 In this specification, standard errors are clustered at the household level.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Column (4), we report the comparison between treatment households that
 received a large transfer and those that received a small tranfer.
 Again excluding pure control households, we evaluate 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\alpha_{v}+\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\beta_{2}S_{vh}+\delta_{1}y_{vh\{i\}B}+\delta_{2}M_{vh\{i\}B}+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:LS_compare}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{vh}$
\end_inset

 is an indicator variable taking the value of 1 if household 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is a spillover household.
 Here the omitted category is households that received small transfers.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (reported in column 4) is the difference in the outcome for households
 that received large transfers versus those that received small transfers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Columns (5) and (6), we report the across-village treatment effect for
 large and small transfers.
 In this analysis, we include pure control households and evaluate: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{vh\{i\}E}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}T_{vh}^{\text{Lg}}+\beta_{2}T_{vh}^{\text{S}}+\beta_{3}S_{v}h+\varepsilon_{vh\{i\}E}\label{eq:LS_across}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, the omitted category is pure control households.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 (column 5) identifies the effect of a monthly transfer using an across-village
 comparison.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 (column 6) identifies the effect of a lump-sum transfer using an across-village
 comparison.
 Note that standard errors are clustered at the village level in this analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Self vs.
 spouse recipients 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In most analyses, we consider the individuals within a household to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

treated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 if that household received a transfer.
 However, there may be differences in effect when an individual receives
 the transfer himself or herself and when an individual's spouse receives
 the transfer.
 In this case, we restrict the regression in equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:MF_within"

\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:MF_across"

\end_inset

 first to male respondents and then to female respondents.
 Thus, we will be able to determine whether the treatment effect differs
 by the gender of the recipient for male and female respondents.
 
\end_layout

